Z||000003||Patrick||08-30-2000||09:35 PM||||<BR>No||||reg||6
Z||000004||JohnnyK||08-30-2000||10:06 PM||||I bled it with fluid till I could see the new stuff coming out at the slave (about 1 bottle of brake fluid). I didn't notice any improvment (but for some reason there was no fluid in the reservoir before that, but it's not leaking, because it is still there). Any other ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||08-31-2000||06:58 AM||||The res. being empty is a sure sign that something is amiss. Check for wetness on the floor mat under the pedal(leaky MC). Search the Forum for bleeding proceedures or <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>   he has one method. Bleeding the clutch is a little differrent from doing the brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cowans||08-31-2000||03:14 PM||||After doing extensive research on this Clutch subject I can suggest logging in to the Fiero Online Service Guide "http://members.xoom.com/_XMCM/fierov6/OSG/clutch-bushings.html. It will explain alot of the Fiero's clutch woes.... certainly one that destroyed my clutch. Good Luck, Sandy||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||08-31-2000||07:08 PM||||Well, the fluid is still in there though, so maybe it evaporated (or something) over the years it was sitting. Where would the typical fluid leak come from. Doesn't look like any is coming out of the cylinders or lines.||||reg||1
