Z||000010||JohnnyK||09-01-2000||06:26 PM||||Well, after reading Sandy's suggestion (go see the clutch problems in the Online Service guide, it appears that the clutch fork bushings could be at fault? Is this common, and does this sound like my problem? If so, is it possible to fix with the tranny still in the car? If not, is it easier to just take out the tranny than to drop the whole cradle (I just want to sell this car to buy a GT, so I need a quick way.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000011||RedOktober||09-01-2000||07:03 PM||||Johnny, <P>I have the same problem for the last year in my daily runner. I still have NOT figured it out yet. I just learned to shift without the clutch...After 1st gear of course.  <P>This problem has been with me since I bought the car 3 years ago. <P>Tried the bleed thing, tried the new master, slave and new clutch kit... so far its all the same. BTW I have the metal pedals. <P>So If there are anyone who can help .... I'm patiently awaiting an answer. <P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)||||reg||1
Z||000012||JohnnyK||09-01-2000||07:31 PM||||Got a bad feeling it's inside the tranny, and it's going to be a big pain in the ass. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||RedOktober||09-01-2000||07:37 PM||||Johnny, <BR>Is your car a 4 cyl with the plastic cup in the clutch leaver that sits in the trans? Someone suggested to me that the plastic could be worn out or it could be a bent fork.  <P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)||||reg||1
Z||000014||Sillhouette.com||09-01-2000||07:52 PM||||If you can shift the tranny while the engine is off (all gears) than there is nothing wrong with your tranny.  Now your clutch is a different story.  When my clutch fluid disappeared I have found thta it leaked out through a bad seal by the actuator on top of the transmission.  Not being able to shift means that your slave or master the piece that has the container attached to is worn to the point where it cannot hold pressure.  With clutch and brakes my best advice is don't mess around with it and replace both your master and slave cylinder.  Preferably a new unit but if finance is an issue you can try a rebuilt one.  That is how mine got fixed.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnnyK||09-01-2000||08:47 PM||||But the fluid is staying there now. It is not leaking. I read an article that describes my problem and it seems to be the bent clutch fork. Is this easy to replace? Also, I heard that contrary to popular belief, the cylinders don't; go bad (well, they do, but it is rare). Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000016||ur2biteme||09-02-2000||08:02 AM||||here are the problems i ran into in order of fixing:<P>trust me, those cylinders go.  they do.  bear in mind to, that these cars are 12 years old at newest, and that is plenty of time for stuff to break loose.  it's possible some debris from the system might have lodged in wherever your leak is. check for a section on the line that seems MUCH dirtier and possibly moist (showing a slow leak that you couldn't tell from m.c. levels).  any opening will allow air in the system.  i had a leaky m.c. on my car at 117k when i bought it, but it still maintained clutch pressure until about 145k.<P>i noticed that there was a leak in the front braided section of hose for the clutch line.  these clutch lines are roughly 13 1/2 feet long.  that gives room for leaks somewhere eventually.    <P>now, the last thing.  steel pedals bend too.  not as often, but mine bent.  i wondered why i couldn't get my clutch to work when my slave wasn't leaking and i had a new master.  i ignored the clutch pedal bending problem that was mentioned for a long time because the steel ones were mentioned to be the ideal ones.  later, on a hunch i went to the local Pic-n-pull yard.  they didn't have any post '86 models, but they had an 85 in immaculate condition (i have no friggin idea why it was in there, lots of new parts on it....) with a new looking aluminum one.  put the aluminum one in and instantly had a clutch.  before you go digging in your tranny, i'd tell you to at least replace the pedal.  you can get one cheap, and it's a lot easier than dropping the cradle.<P>this is the ordeal i went through.  i'd suggest inspecting and replacing any of the items mentioned before dropping the cradle.  chances are that you'll have to do it anyway eventually.  just because these cylinders are said to be indestructable, doesn't mean they are.  the titanic was said to be unsinkable  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  the clutch line is rediculously long, and i don't know what's up with the pedal design on these cars.  there is probably nothing wrong with your tranny.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||2birds||09-02-2000||09:16 AM||||A Mity-Vac goes a long way toward mmaking bleeding brakes and clutches simpler, especially without an assistant.  Well worth the $25 at Wally World.  You can swap out the entire system worth of fluid in a few minutes.  Make sure your bleeder screws aren't allowing air past their threads, though.  A very small amount of grease on new bleeder screws usually works, but I haven't been able to get one for my clutch slave cylinder without buying a complete rebuild kit at the minimum.  I just went for a complete new slave instead.  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||JohnnyK||09-02-2000||07:27 PM||||Well, aparently the bent forks inside the tranny usually go, especially on early Fieros.. (Mines an 85). Hopefully it IS a cylinder, but how can I check these? Also, I have the steel pedal on there, thats the first thing I replaced. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||09-03-2000||03:36 PM||||just to clarify, DOT3 brake fluid won't evaporate out of the master cyl tank. It went some place.<P>The fact it appears to be holding the fluid now doesn't mean it's not still screwy. Even loosing a fraction of a drop can be enough to keep it from working right. check under the dash for fluid. If there's any trace of fluid on the master cyl it has leaked.<P>Since the tank was dry likely you have to do whatever "bench" bleed the master may call for. You can slave bleed all day and still not get all the air out of the master untill it's bench bled.<P>You can also have an air leak. That's when the system lets air into the line with no apparent fluid loss. There are a couple things that can do that. Mostly it happens when air is sucked past either the slave or master cyl piston seals because the slave is binding and creating a vacuum in the system.||||reg||1
Z||000020||JohnnyK||09-03-2000||03:49 PM||||So what your saying is it's probably not in the transmission? Again, I thought that it was common for the forks or whatever to bend in the 5 spd isuzu? Guess I am going to have to find a fluid leak. Any way to check the cylinders?||||reg||1
Z||000021||JohnnyK||09-03-2000||03:49 PM||||So what your saying is it's probably not in the transmission? Again, I thought that it was common for the forks or whatever to bend in the 5 spd isuzu? Guess I am going to have to find a fluid leak. Any way to check the cylinders?||||reg||1
Z||000022||JohnnyK||09-03-2000||07:46 PM||||Well, I just took apart the master right now. Every thing looks like it checks out. Seals don't look really worn at all, and aside from some mud or something on the inside of the boot (I don't know what it was. Just looked like mud..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Everything looks good. I think I am going to replace the steel lines and see what happens, because I know my front brakes were leaking, and the lines finally blew. How do I bench bleed the master?||||reg||1
Z||000023||lowCG||09-03-2000||07:56 PM||||Well,I made it to Seattle all the way from Ford country(Detroit),had great fun with the newly installed 3.4ohc motor,blew away a few cars in Montana...<BR>but,my clutch has developed the same symptoms described here and I've vacume     bled,re-bushed the pedal,replaced the hydraulics with little results.The next thing I might try is to re-bench bleed the master cylinder.I also am now questioningI the new slave cylinder which lets air into the system whenever I press the actuator rod back into the cylinder while trying to bleed from that end of the system.I'm getting only 3/4" of movement at the clutch arm,up from the 5/8" I started with.Whew.                               ||||reg||8
Z||000024||JohnnyK||09-04-2000||01:41 AM||||So, how DO you bench bleed?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||lowCG||09-04-2000||11:56 AM||||Johnny,a master cylinder can be bench bled by clamping the mounting ear in a vise,then connecting a sealed hose(clear)from the line connection going back into the resevoir,and pumping to get the air bubbles out.The only thing is that when I did this,the bubbles only moved back and forth in the tube,never going away.I finally hooked up the vacuum bleeder that I got and by slowly moving the piston back and forth while bleeding,got rid of the bubbles.It didn't make any difference in the clutch action however.I've had it with this fussy,crappy system,never had any problems like this on other cars with hydraulic clutches,and will either take the car to a commercial brake shop,or replace the slave cylinder with a smaller bore unit from another vehicle,since my results have been consistent.||||reg||1
